nodecode-optmem is a rewrite of rarf's rarf/optmem-hermes-plugin:

  https://github.com/rarf/optmem-hermes-plugin
  commit 2868af6620bf07958f833ca354fbeecf2a56b265
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 OptMem Hermes Plugin contributors (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's memory engine, as Common Lisp in engine.lisp
and cell.lisp — the store's fixed-width on-disk format (LOG.txt records of
320 bytes, TREE/<size> summaries of 288 bytes, in the layout Victor Taelin's
`memo` tool and this plugin share), the binary decay tree and its cover, the
naps that write one-line compressions, the wake digest with its completeness
report, the regex / BM25 / auto recall modes with accent folding and the
literal-substring fallback, the per-store size knobs, the model-facing import
of dated lines, and the deterministic local summarizer. The eight tools keep
their names, descriptions, parameters and JSON answers (optmem_note,
optmem_recall, optmem_wake, optmem_nap, optmem_zoom, optmem_forget,
optmem_config, optmem_import), and the words the model is given about them
keep the original's warnings: one atomic fact a line, compress when asked,
never edit the store by hand.

What was left out, and why: the optional LLM summary through the host's
auxiliary task (the local extractor is the original's default path and needs
no model, no key and no egress, which is what a cell with no provider of its
own can promise); the Hermes CLI verbs (status, migrate, mode, rollback,
check, init) — the cell's config section, /optmem and the tools give the
same surface, and a cell has no command line of its own; the Hybrid versus
OptMem-only mode switch that rewrites a host's config.yaml after migrating
native MEMORY.md files, which is Hermes's memory layout and not Nodecode's;
and the optmem_init tool, because the store is created when the cell starts.
The Python package, its tests and its Desktop config-panel schema are not
carried over; the rewrite is Lisp against Nodecode's cell contract.
